#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d7e69ba-dfb4-54f1-967b-f46bb90aec38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289548} {:answer/id #uuid "782a60a8-7501-5dba-b015-682ebcab6500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d7e69ba-dfb4-54f1-967b-f46bb90aec38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289548} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8179b9-1599-5443-89d6-6c9a50d5f03f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11c26dab-b087-5e3e-b280-6de12a9fd881" :question/math-academy-id "q-351439" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010979/assets/0b9c4ebc430e05ef205e3d8108f2e1649bbc51b1a6b54e8f47b48e7ddad1e9a6.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y=4{sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{cos}^{3}⁡t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{sin}^{3}⁡t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =12{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{2}+{(12{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t+144{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}12|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
